- The Art Gallery wishes to maintain data on their customers, artists and
paintings. They may have several paintings by each artist in the gallery at one
time.
- Paintings may be bought and sold several times. In other words, the gallery may
sell a painting, then buy it back at a later date and sell it to another
customer.
- Customers want to be able to look up paintings by type (i.e. impressionist, abstract, etc.). Subject matter can also be there.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Customers (CustomerID, EmailId, FirstName, LastName)
Artists (ArtistID, ArtistName)
Paintings (PaintingID, ArtistID, PaintingName, Type, SubjectMatter, isAvailable) - Add index on Type,
Buybacks (BuybackID, PaintingID, CustomerId, BuybackDate, BuybackAmount)
Sales (SaleID, CustomerID, PaintingID, SaleDate, SaleAmount)

=====================================================================================

Questions:
* Write a query that returns all the paintings by Artist X sold between date 1
  and date 2.
* Write a query that returns the painting that has been sold the most.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

SELECT Paintings.*
FROM Paintings
JOIN Sales ON Paintings.PaintingID = Sales.PaintingID
JOIN Artists ON Paintings.ArtistID = Artists.ArtistID
WHERE Artists.ArtistName = 'Artist X'
  AND Sales.SaleDate BETWEEN 'date1' AND 'date2';

SELECT Paintings.*, COUNT(Sales.PaintingID) AS SalesCount
FROM Paintings
JOIN Sales ON Paintings.PaintingID = Sales.PaintingID
GROUP BY Paintings.PaintingID
ORDER BY SalesCount DESC
LIMIT 1;
